title='Planning New Urban Hubs in Western Democratic Republic of the Congo through Conditional Location Optimization and an Assessment Framework for Functional Autonomy'
d.paragraphs[1].text=title
for x in d.paragraphs[1].runs:x.font.name='Times New Roman';x.font.size=Pt(16);x.bold=True
replace(d,'Abstract—','Abstract—This study investigates where additional urban hubs could improve territorial integration across Greater Bandundu, Greater Équateur, Kinshasa and Kongo Central. A directed October 2026 OpenStreetMap road graph and complete WorldPop and GHS-POP population grids support conditional location allocation. Twenty-six existing hubs remain fixed; three, five or ten new service centres are selected to maximize additional modelled population within 100 km. Candidate grids of 10 and 25 km use a documented protected-area mask and point-sampled land-cover exclusions. Twenty-four new optimizations compare population products, road-class sets, candidate grids and an optional 15-km water-proximity constraint. All terminate with zero optimality gap. With WorldPop and main roads, five new centres increase five-kilometre-demand coverage from 59.08% to 64.68% on the 25-km candidate grid and to 65.57% on the 10-km grid. At ten new centres on the coarse grid, coverage reaches 69.17%. These conditional gains complement earlier network and population sensitivity experiments. Functional autonomy is operationalized as an assessment framework separating measured accessibility proxies from unmeasured productive, energy, service and trade functions. Water proximity is tested as a resource-access condition, not evidence of reliable supply. Selected centres identify sectors for further investigation, not cleared construction parcels or proven sovereign cities. Historical inheritance remains contextual; no colonial causal effect is estimated. Household-access validation, operational transport verification, land rights, investment costs and functional-autonomy observations remain required before implementation.')
replace(d,'Keywords—','Keywords—new urban hubs; conditional location allocation; functional autonomy; territorial integration; population coverage; Democratic Republic of the Congo.')
replace(d,'The study examines Greater Bandundu','The study examines Greater Bandundu, Greater Équateur, Kinshasa, and Kongo Central as one interconnected territorial system. Its ten provinces span the Atlantic interface, the metropolitan concentration around Kinshasa, and substantial interior and northern settlement areas. The planning objective is to locate additional urban hubs that could extend interior service coverage and provide a basis for investigating relative functional autonomy. Accessibility gains are measured conditionally; economic independence, employment and viable urban construction are not inferred from them. Existing-hub analysis supplies the baseline, and new candidate-centre optimization is introduced in Sections II.L–M and III.L–M.')
replace(d,'The revised contribution is','The revised contribution combines an audited baseline with a new-centre location experiment. It compares two population products and road assumptions, then selects screened candidate centres in addition to all 26 existing hubs. The central question is where additional hubs can maximize modelled service coverage, how grid and resource-access assumptions change those solutions, and which further evidence would be necessary to assess functional autonomy. Exact optimization, input checks and an explicit autonomy framework are kept distinct from empirical outcome validation.')
replace(d,'Historical transport development remains','Historical transport development motivates the integration question, but no colonial-era network is digitized or compared with current data here. A causal colonial claim is therefore omitted from the title and findings. The ambition of new urban hubs and relative functional autonomy is retained through conditional location optimization and a specified evidence framework; realized sovereignty is not asserted. This distinction permits territorial planning questions without treating a desired future as an observed result.')
for x in d.paragraphs:
    if x.text.startswith('The existing candidate set contains'):
        x.text=x.text.replace('The existing candidate set','The baseline existing-hub set').replace('No new-city location is optimized in the main revised model.','These 26 hubs remain fixed in the additional-centre experiment; new screened sites form a separate decision set in Section II.L.')
    if x.text.startswith('Neither Yanganga nor Ebale'):
        x.text=x.text.replace('while the proposed sites cease to be planning recommendations.','without endorsing those legacy identities. The new conditional allocation in Section III.L uses a different objective and a complete screened candidate grid.')
