anchor=next(x for x in d.paragraphs if x.text=='III. RESULTS AND DISCUSSION')._p
def methods():
    sub(d,'L. Conditional Allocation of Additional Urban Hubs')
    p(d,'All 26 existing hubs are retained as a fixed service baseline. This assumes comparable service availability and no capacity limits; actual service inventories are unavailable. Let b_i be one if demand cell i lies within 100 km of at least one fixed hub in the specified graph. New candidate set J comprises screened 10- or 25-km grid centres from Section II.H: at least 40 km from the retained hubs, permitted WorldCover point classes 20, 30, 40 or 60, and no full-cell intersection with the protected-area polygons or the reported-area point buffer. This retains 1,583 and 241 centres, respectively. Proximity to the 26 hubs does not establish distance from every town or village. A candidate can represent expansion of an existing rural settlement; neither vacant land nor a legally new city is established.')
    p(d,'Candidate centres attach to the nearest retained road vertex with a geodesic connector at most 5 km; connectors are assumed, not newly designed roads. Demand is the full 5-km grid from each population product, with the same attachment rule. Unattached and unreachable cells remain in the denominator. The candidate set after attachment has 1,291/1,358 centres on the 10-km grid and 201/208 on the 25-km grid for main roads/roads plus tracks. The directed geometry graph and distance threshold are unchanged. Ferries are not added in this experiment, to retain a matched road-only baseline; Section III.K is a separate conditional mode test.')
    p(d,'For a_ij = 1 when cell i can reach new candidate j within 100 km including connectors, maximize Σ_i P_i(1−b_i)y_i subject to y_i ≤ Σ_j a_ij x_j, Σ_j x_j = q, and binary x_j. Covered-demand variables y_i lie in [0,1]; positive weights and binary site choices yield exact covered masses. No preference weights or economic-autonomy score enter this objective. The baseline union is counted once and removed from marginal demand. Demand with identical full candidate-incidence vectors is aggregated by population sum using packed bits; no 26-site encoding limit applies. The reported selected sets are one optimum, with possible ties. Chosen q values are scenarios, not an estimated optimum number of cities; no budget or site-capacity model is available.')
    p(d,'On the 25-km candidate grid, q = 3, 5 and 10 are crossed with two road sets and two population products, giving 12 runs. An additional q = 5 screen requires distance to mapped water at most 15 km, giving four runs. The 10-km grid repeats q = 5 with and without this water constraint across both road sets and products, giving eight runs. The total is 24 new optimizations, distinct from the 408 existing-hub and audit runs. Every objective is recomputed from the original demand weights and selected-site union. This experiment varies candidate grids but holds demand resolution at 5 km; earlier 1/5-km existing-hub sensitivities do not establish fine-grid stability of the new-site solutions. The water threshold is an explicit scenario choice, not an elicited or calibrated standard.')
    sub(d,'M. Functional Autonomy as an Assessment Framework')
    p(d,'Relative functional autonomy denotes the capacity of a hub and its hinterland to satisfy essential functions while exchanging with other territories. It does not imply isolation or national political sovereignty. Urban–rural linkage frameworks examine flows of people, products, services and information [41]; polycentricity has scale-dependent functional meanings and cannot be inferred solely from several selected points [42]. This study treats autonomy as a planning hypothesis requiring separately documented functions, not as an existing composite score.')
    p(d,'The operational evidence framework specifies productive capacity as the share of hinterland output processed locally; energy as hours of dependable service and the capacity to sustain critical uses; services as the proportion of identified health, education and administrative needs met locally; employment as local jobs and work-related travel; exchanges as observed origin–destination links; and dependence as the share of essential supply or services requiring Kinshasa. Their denominators, territories, dates and service categories must be defined before collection. None of these outcomes is estimated from population, illumination or proximity alone. Available population potential, land-cover class and mapped road/water/rail/air distances are reported as contextual proxies only. Missing energy, employment, production and trade observations remain missing; no zero, imputed achievement or sovereignty threshold is substituted.')
    p(d,'The optional water constraint connects one resource-access consideration to the location model without an arbitrary weighted index. Distance to a waterway cannot establish potable water, dry-season yield, flood safety or navigability. Evaluating a new-city scenario would require engineering and financial designs for production, utilities, services and connections, followed by feasibility and rights assessments. Realized autonomy would require subsequent observations; any simulation of future autonomy must remain conditional on separately specified investment assumptions.')
before(d,anchor,methods)

anchor=next(x for x in d.paragraphs if x.text=='IV. CONCLUSION')._p
def findings():
    sub(d,'L. New Urban Hub Location Results')
    p(d,'All 24 new-centre optimizations terminate with solver status zero and optimality gap zero; recomputed unions match the objective. Their baseline differs from the earlier ten-selected-hub result: here all 26 existing hubs are fixed, and demand resolution is 5 km. Thus WorldPop main-road baseline coverage is 59.08%, not 50.97%; the corresponding GHS-POP value is 68.04%. These are assumed service-distance coverages, not observations of functioning services.')
    table(d,'XIX','Additional centres on the 25 km candidate grid',['Road set','Product','q','Coverage %','Gain pp'],[['Tracks' if v.tracks else 'Main','WP' if v['product']=='WorldPop' else 'GHS',v.new_hubs,f'{v.with_new_hubs_pct:.2f}',f'{v.gain_pp:.2f}'] for _,v in r[(r.grid_km==25)&(r.condition=='screened')].iterrows()],[16,16,8,23,23])
    p(d,'With WorldPop and main roads, three, five and ten additional centres increase coverage to 62.59%, 64.68% and 69.17%, equivalent to marginal gains of 3.52, 5.61 and 10.09 percentage points. Five centres add 2.43 million modelled inhabitants to coverage; they do not create that population or predict migration. Including tracks raises the five-centre marginal gain to 6.77 points. Population-product and graph changes affect the attainable benefit, so one solution cannot be presented as a unique territorial optimum.')
    table(d,'XX','Five new centres across candidate grids',['Road set','Product','25 km %','10 km %'],[['Tracks' if tr else 'Main','WP' if prod=='WorldPop' else 'GHS',f'{scenario(25,tr,prod).with_new_hubs_pct:.2f}',f'{scenario(10,tr,prod).with_new_hubs_pct:.2f}'] for tr in [False,True] for prod in ['WorldPop','GHSPOP']],[18,18,25,25])
    p(d,'On the 10-km candidate grid, five centres reach 65.57% WorldPop main-road coverage, a gain of 6.49 points or 2.81 million modelled inhabitants beyond the fixed baseline. Its gain is 0.88 point above the coarse-grid gain. The corresponding GHS-POP gain rises from 4.46 to 5.34 points. With tracks, finer-grid gains are 7.32 points for WorldPop and 5.67 for GHS-POP. These grids are not nested candidate subsets, so monotonicity is not guaranteed generally; the observed improvement is specific to these runs. The 25-km strict screen omits Équateur, and point-level land cover does not establish the suitability of an entire grid cell.')
    pic=d.add_paragraph();pic.alignment=WD_ALIGN_PARAGRAPH.CENTER;pic.paragraph_format.first_line_indent=Mm(0);pic.add_run().add_picture(str(O/'new_hub_grid_map.png'),width=Inches(3.36));pic.paragraph_format.keep_with_next=True
    cap=p(d,'Fig. 6. One optimal five-new-centre set for WorldPop and main roads on each candidate grid, with all 26 existing hubs fixed. N1–N5 refer to Table XXI. Coordinates locate sampled sectors, not construction parcels. Boundary: geoBoundaries; optimization inputs: OSM, WorldPop, WorldCover and protected-area screening.');cap.paragraph_format.first_line_indent=Mm(0)
    for run in cap.runs:run.font.size=Pt(8)
    t=sites(10)
    table(d,'XXI','One optimal five centre set on the 10 km grid',['Site','Province','Lon °','Lat °','Water km'],[['N'+str(i+1),v.province,f'{v.longitude:.3f}',f'{v.latitude:.3f}',f'{v.d_water_m/1000:.2f}'] for i,v in t.iterrows()],[9,25,17,17,18])
    p(d,'The identifiers in the supplementary table retain exact input coordinates and grid indices. Changing grids changes selected sectors: the five-centre WorldPop main-road set spans Kwilu, Sud-Ubangi and Mongala on the 10-km grid, compared with Kwango, Kwilu, Sud-Ubangi and Nord-Ubangi on the 25-km grid. No province quota is imposed. This concentration reflects the marginal coverage objective and screened opportunities; it does not justify excluding other provinces from investment. Optimality is conditional on the candidate set and encoded distances, not on urban viability, public costs or provincial equity.')
    sub(d,'M. Resource Conditions and Functional Autonomy Interpretation')
    p(d,'The optional mapped-water constraint reduces WorldPop five-centre coverage from 64.68% to 64.58% on the coarse main-road grid, and from 65.57% to 65.43% on the fine grid. The respective losses are 0.11 and 0.14 point, with different selected identities possible. For GHS-POP and main roads, the coarse loss is 0.03 point and the fine-grid optimal objective is unchanged. Across all matched road/product/grid comparisons the largest loss is 0.26 point. This quantifies an explicit coverage–resource-proximity trade-off; it neither proves water sufficiency nor ranks normative priorities.')
    p(d,'N1 and N2 in the fine-grid unconstrained WorldPop example lie approximately 23.87 and 24.49 km from mapped water. Their access advantage therefore cannot be interpreted as water autonomy; an implementation dossier would need a source and supply design. N3–N5 are within 15 km, but still lack measured water quality, reliable energy, production, jobs and service capacity. None is labelled a proven sovereign city. Their role is to identify sectors where a new hub or settlement upgrade could serve otherwise uncovered modelled demand, and where functional investments could be investigated.')
    p(d,'The new-centre module restores the planning question without reviving the old arbitrary preference ranking. Historical network orientation remains relevant context, while a causal colonial attribution is outside the executed design. Likewise, a coverage gain is not evidence that trade dependence on Kinshasa decreases. Productive and service functions, interprovincial exchanges and governance must be specified and observed before that conclusion. The principal validation gap remains household/service-access observations compatible with the same modes, dates and locations; exact solver gaps do not close it.')
before(d,anchor,findings)
replace(d,'Replacing selected-node demand','Conditional new-centre allocation extends the original territorial ambition beyond selecting existing hubs. Keeping all 26 existing hubs fixed, five additional centres on the coarse grid increase WorldPop main-road five-kilometre-demand coverage from 59.08% to 64.68%; the finer candidate grid reaches 65.57%. Ten coarse-grid centres reach 69.17%. These are exact optima for the encoded candidate sets and assumed 100-km access threshold, not established construction plans. Product, road and candidate-grid choices materially affect the solutions.')
replace(d,'The audits change interpretation','New urban hubs remain the planning objective, and relative functional autonomy is retained as a specified assessment framework. The model locates sectors with conditional marginal service benefits and evaluates one explicit water-proximity constraint. It does not establish reliable utilities, local transformation, employment, observed trade independence or parcel feasibility. Historical causation is not estimated. The previous input and sensitivity audits remain necessary: mapped connectivity, population modelling and assumed connectors constrain interpretation. Twenty-four new-centre runs complement 408 previous optimizations. Neither the optimum count of cities nor their realized sovereignty is demonstrated. Matched access observations, operational verification, rights and cost studies, and functional data are required before implementation; the main new-centre objective does not depend on an unavailable expert panel.')
for text in ['[41] UN-Habitat, Urban-Rural Linkages: Guiding Principles and Framework for Action to Advance Integrated Territorial Development. Nairobi, 2019. Available: https://unhabitat.org/urban-rural-linkages-guiding-principles.','[42] M. Brezzi and P. Veneri, “Assessing Polycentric Urban Systems in the OECD: Country, Regional and Metropolitan Perspectives,” OECD Regional Development Working Papers, No. 2014/01, 2014, doi: 10.1787/5jz5mpdkmvnr-en.']:
    x=d.add_paragraph(text);x.paragraph_format.first_line_indent=Mm(0)
    for run in x.runs:run.font.name='Times New Roman';run.font.size=Pt(8)
